        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p><strong>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First)</strong> is a link-state Interior Gateway Protocol used heavily in enterprise networks.</p>
            
            <h3>How OSPF Works</h3>
            <p>Unlike simple distance-vector protocols, OSPF routers build a complete topological map of the network (the link-state database) by exchanging link-state advertisements (LSAs). Each router then runs the Shortest Path First (SPF) algorithm, also known as Dijkstra's algorithm, to calculate the best paths to all destinations within its autonomous system.</p>
            
            <h3>Key Concepts</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Autonomous System Context:</strong> OSPF operates within a single routing domain (Interior Gateway Protocol).</li>
                <li><strong>Areas:</strong> OSPF uses a hierarchical design. All areas must connect to the backbone area, known as <strong>Area 0</strong>.</li>
                <li><strong>Neighbors and Adjacency:</strong> Routers discover each other via Hello packets. Once they agree on parameters, they form an adjacency and exchange link-state information.</li>
                <li><strong>Cost / Metric:</strong> OSPF path selection is determined by <em>cost</em>. It does NOT automatically choose the path with the fewest routers. Cost is typically derived from interface bandwidth, though vendors calculate default interface costs differently.</li>
                <li><strong>Route Convergence:</strong> When a link fails, OSPF quickly floods the updated state to all routers, which recalculate their SPF trees, resulting in fast convergence.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Simple Topology Example</h3>
            <pre><code>   Router A
      |
   Router B
  /        \
R-C       R-D</code></pre>
            <p>If R-C and R-D both connect to the same destination network, Router B will select the path with the lowest cumulative OSPF cost.</p>
            
            <h3>OSPFv2 vs OSPFv3</h3>
            <p><strong>OSPFv2</strong> is typically used for IPv4 routing. <strong>OSPFv3</strong> was originally designed to support <a href="ipv6-fundamentals.html">IPv6</a>, but has evolved to support multiple address families (both IPv4 and IPv6) within its modern framing.</p>
        
        </div>
        
